00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:24.000
<v Orlando>So, rule number one is whenever a word
begins with an S, it sounds like an S sound.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.350
<v Orlando>Rule number two, and Valdo already
mentioned this rule when we started.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.170
<v Orlando>If an S is found between vowels, it'll sound like a Z.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.000
<v Orlando>We already heard that with José Luis's name.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.300
<v Valdo>As in <lang pt-BR>José</lang>.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.550
<v Orlando>But we also hear it in the word…

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Casa</lang>,

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.050
<v Orlando>"house", and…

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>coisa</lang>, "thing". <lang pt-BR>Visita</lang>.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.710
<v Orlando>And each of those words will sound like an S in Spanish.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.800
<v Orlando>So José Luis, how would you say again the word house?

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.750
<v José Luis><lang es>Casa.</lang>

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:54.300
<v José Luis><lang es>José.</lang>

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Casa.</lang>

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>José.</lang>

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.300
<v José Luis><lang es>Cosa.</lang>

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:58.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Coisa.</lang>

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.040
<v Orlando>So this is a perfect rule, and it's very easy to follow.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.620
<v Orlando>Between vowels, your S should sound like a Z.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.550
<v Orlando>And notice how different that sounds from the Spanish word.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.290
<v José Luis><lang es>Cosa.</lang>

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.000
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Coisa.</lang>

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.730
<v Orlando>Rule number three is an interesting
rule. It's an orthographic rule.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:22.500
<v Orlando>It basically is if you have an S between vowels
and you want it to sound like an S, what do you do?

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:24.340
<v Valdo>It's double S.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:24.890
<v Michelle>Double S.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:25.800
<v Orlando>You write it with two S's.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:30.850
<v Orlando>And so the word <lang pt-BR>passar</lang> is P-A-S-S-A-R.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.800
<v Orlando>And that means, I know it's between
vowels, but we really do want an S sound.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.800
<v Orlando>The word <lang pt-BR>massa</lang>,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.550
<v Valdo>M-A-S-S-A.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.500
<v Orlando>And the word <lang pt-BR>assim</lang>,

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.300
<v Valdo>A-S-S-I-M.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:52.084
<v Orlando>Right, and so it's just a tricky orthographic way to say we know
that normally an S between vowels sounds like a Z, but we really,

00:06:52.084 --> 00:06:54.260
<v Orlando>really do want it to sound like an S here.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.050
<v Orlando>And we're going to prove it by putting two S's together.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.300
<v Michelle>Two S's.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:01.580
<v Orlando>So rule number one, at the beginning
of a word, S always sounds like an S.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.080
<v Orlando>Rule number two, between vowels it sounds like a Z.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.000
<v Orlando>Rule number three, if you really want to have it sound like an S,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:09.390
<v Valdo>Double S.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.210
<v Michelle>Double S.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.800
<v Orlando>With two S's.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:19.752
<v Orlando>Before we talk about rules four and five, we don't want to
get too technical here, we've got to take a little side road.

00:07:19.752 --> 00:07:28.050
<v Orlando>because this talks about how an S sounds when the S is found at
the end of a word or that S is found at the end of a syllable.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:33.500
<v Orlando>And so this is a strange rule. We have to know
now when we call this syllable final position.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:35.000
<v Orlando>Sorry about this.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:40.250
<v Orlando>And the rule is, related to a thing called voicing.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.300
<v Orlando>And everybody listening, you can try this little experiment.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.161
<v Orlando>There are tons of consonants where
the only difference between them is

00:07:48.161 --> 00:07:52.080
<v Orlando>that sometimes your vocal cords are
vibrating and sometimes they're not.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:08:00.300
<v Orlando>In fact, if you hold your fingers against your Adam's apple and
just say "sss", you don't feel any vibration in your vocal cords.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:04.550
<v Orlando>But if you change it to a Z sound, "zzz", you do.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:10.750
<v Orlando>And you can alternate: "sss", "zzz". And that is the voicing.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.503
<v Orlando>I know that sounds technical, but the rule is,

00:08:14.503 --> 00:08:21.759
<v Orlando>if your S at the end of a syllable is before another
consonant which has the voicing, your S will have voicing too.

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:28.006
<v Orlando>And if your S at the end of a syllable doesn't have
voicing, then it will stay without the voicing.

00:08:28.006 --> 00:08:34.000
<v Orlando>Okay, so then the next step is, I need to learn
which sounds have voicing and which ones don't.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:45.300
<v Orlando>A lot of them do. So for example, if you
have M, N, R, L, B, D, G, they're all voiced,

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.980
<v Orlando>and so your S becomes voiced as well, becoming a Z.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:53.891
<v Orlando>And if you have words with a T, or a
P, or a K, or a CH, they're not voiced.

00:08:53.891 --> 00:09:00.864
<v Orlando>So it might be better to go through rules four and five
now, and hopefully that was not way too off the mark.

00:09:00.864 --> 00:09:08.156
<v Orlando>So rule number four is, if you have an S at the end of a
syllable before a voiced consonant, it'll sound like a Z.

00:09:08.156 --> 00:09:12.250
<v Orlando>And so a couple of examples here, Michelle, would be words like…

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.550
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Lisboa, Osvaldo, mesmo, desde...</lang>

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:24.490
<v Orlando>And so in every one of those cases, the S sounds
like a Z because it comes before a voiced consonant.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:26.500
<v Orlando>Let's have Valdo say those same words.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:32.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Lisboa, Osvaldo, mesmo, desde...</lang>

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.230
<v Orlando>This rule you will notice that even in
Spanish there's a little bit of that.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:40.770
<v Orlando>You know, if I say the word <lang es>mismo</lang>, it
sounds almost like a Z, doesn't it?

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.150
<v Orlando>It's not totally an "S".

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.750
<v Orlando>And that's because your vocal cords are vibrating
and so it just starts the "S" to vibrate too.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.310
<v Orlando>This rule is so powerful it even happens between words.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:58.630
<v Orlando>And so if I say, for example, <lang pt-BR>os meninos</lang>, "the
