00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:05.300
<v Orlando>But if I add another word, like "the beautiful boys"
or "the handsome boys", that would come out as…

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:06.550
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Os meninos bonitos.</lang>

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.250
<v Orlando>And then it sounds like a Z through <lang pt-BR>os meninos bonitos</lang>.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:12.840
<v Orlando>That's rule number four.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:20.400
<v Orlando>Rule number five is if that S at the end of a syllable
is before a voiceless consonant, it'll stay voiceless.

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:23.250
<v Orlando>And so now we have words, for example, Valdo.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:29.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Está, esposa, esforço, esquece.</lang>

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.750
<v Orlando>And Michelle, the same words?

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.050
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Está, esposa, esforço, esquece.</lang>

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.910
<v Orlando>Great, and so that S continues to sound like an S.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.950
<v Orlando>And again, that happens between words.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.800
<v Orlando>If I want to say "the happy tigers", for example, that would be?

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.410
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Os tigres felizes.</lang>

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.300
<v Michelle>That's what came to mind.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.800
<v Orlando>Okay.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:57.799
<v Orlando>So, that is a lot of stuff, and I know we've gone through
a lot here, but it's a very important aspect of Portuguese.

00:10:57.799 --> 00:11:00.710
<v Orlando>Non-native speakers are pronouncing too many S's,

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:05.045
<v Orlando>and one of the things that we go back
